WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.460
How does longest common subsequence

2
00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.620
work? We will derive its

3
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.840
two recurrence cases, fill a

4
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.400
complete prefix table, reconstruct an

5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.700
actual sequence, and count both

6
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.440
the table work and the

7
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.580
backtracking cost.

8
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.500
A subsequence preserves order but

9
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.920
may skip characters, whereas a

10
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.340
substring must be contiguous. We

11
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.960
seek a sequence appearing in

12
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.840
both inputs with maximum length,

13
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.840
and more than one answer

14
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.460
can sometimes attain that length.

15
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.620
Let L at i and

16
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.100
j mean the best common-subsequence

17
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.060
length of the first i

18
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.560
characters of X and the

19
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.320
first j characters of Y.

20
00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:40.540
If either prefix is empty,

21
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.140
its common-subsequence length is zero,

22
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.080
giving the zero border of

23
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:45.580
our table.

24
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.760
If the terminal characters match,

25
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.980
there exists an optimum using

26
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.860
that common terminal character. Append

27
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.000
it to an optimum for

28
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.100
the two shorter prefixes, so

29
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.460
the value is one plus

30
00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:58.720
the diagonal predecessor.

31
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.500
If the terminal characters differ,

32
00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.380
they cannot both be the

33
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:04.840
final character of a common

34
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.280
subsequence. Omit one or the

35
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.620
other and take the larger

36
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:09.960
value from the upper and

37
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.700
left neighbours, covering both possibilities.

38
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.960
Fill rows from top to

39
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.560
bottom, and columns from left

40
00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.440
to right, so every dependency

41
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.100
is ready. For the first

42
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.900
X character A, the four

43
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.440
values against Y are one,

44
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.580
one, one, one, because the

45
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.300
matched A remains available as

46
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.440
the Y prefix grows.

47
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.600
For the next X character

48
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.920
C, the first value stays

49
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.200
one, and matching C raises

50
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.640
the second value to two.

51
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.180
The rest of this row

52
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.400
stays two, giving one, two,

53
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:41.360
two, two.

54
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.800
For the first G, the

55
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.660
row becomes one, two, two,

56
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.880
three. The final entry uses

57
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.300
the matching G in Y

58
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.580
and adds one to the

59
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.160
diagonal value two, establishing a

60
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.980
common subsequence of length three.

61
00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.320
The second G does not

62
00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.200
produce a fourth common character,

63
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.340
because the Y prefix contains

64
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.840
only one G at its

65
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.760
end. Its row is one,

66
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.400
two, two, three again, and

67
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.780
the final A row has

68
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.780
those same values, leaving the

69
00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.840
optimum at three.

70
00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.880
To reconstruct, begin at the

71
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.820
bottom-right cell, and choose the

72
00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.700
upper neighbour when an unequal-character

73
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.920
tie occurs. From the final

74
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.460
A we move up; at

75
00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:23.640
the second G we move

76
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:26.660
diagonally, emit G, and continue

77
00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:27.860
at the shorter prefixes.

78
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.240
Continue upward past the unused

79
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.660
first G, then left past

80
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.760
T, reaching the matching C.

81
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.080
Emit C diagonally, then emit

82
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.800
the matching A, and stop

83
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.720
when an empty-prefix border is

84
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:41.100
reached.

85
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.700
The emitted characters are reversed

86
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.080
because we travelled from the

87
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.320
end toward the start. Reverse

88
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:48.840
G, C, A to obtain

89
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.020
A, C, G, which appears

90
00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.460
in order in both original

91
00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:54.220
strings and has the optimal

92
00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.020
length three.

93
00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.120
There are n times m

94
00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.040
interior states with constant work

95
00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.040
per state, so filling costs

96
00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.560
Theta of n m and

97
00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:03.980
the full table occupies big

98
00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:05.240
O of n m space.

99
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.620
Each backtracking step decreases at

100
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.480
least one index, giving big

101
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.860
O of n plus m

102
00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:11.860
reconstruction time.

103
00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:15.440
Memoised top-down recursion also avoids

104
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.280
repeated states, so it can

105
00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.080
achieve the same polynomial state

106
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.060
bound. The slide warning about

107
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.300
inefficient recursion concerns the uncached

108
00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.680
version; optional rolling rows save

109
00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.020
length-only storage but complicate this

110
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:28.700
simple reconstruction.

111
00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:31.080
Things to take note of:

112
00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.620
define prefix lengths, initialise both

113
00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.840
borders, distinguish equal and unequal

114
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.920
terminal cases, and state your

115
00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.380
tie rule. Recover a sequence

116
00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:43.740
as well as its length

117
00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.300
when asked, and reverse backtracked

118
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.560
characters instead of proudly submitting

119
00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.080
the answer backwards, how charming.
